# Phase 3 final audit continuation — 20260928T080155Z

- Continued source-level audit of inbound processor dispatch against Java 5.5.1 `NettyRemotingAbstract.processRequestCommand`: executor submission `RejectedExecutionException` maps to SYSTEM_BUSY, but other `Throwable` is caught without a response. Rust had treated a panic from `RequestExecutor::execute` as rejection. Changed Rust to return SYSTEM_BUSY only for `Ok(Err(_))`; a submission panic is contained without masquerading as a Java rejection.
- Added scripted regression `p3_processor_executor_panic_does_not_masquerade_as_rejection`; executor rejection coverage remains in `p3_processor_specific_executors_route_or_reject_tasks`. Both focused tests pass. `cargo fmt --all --check` initially identified formatting in the new assert; ran `cargo fmt --all`.
- Updated inventory with the exact executor rejection/panic distinction and test disposition. An exhaustive reference scan found stale test IDs; corrected the allocator test index and added the executor panic case to compatibility/test indexes.
- Reviewed complete English and Traditional Chinese Phase 3 plans. P3-V01/V02/V03/U01 are local evidence only; P3-V04 remains an explicit Phase 19 handoff and is not a Phase 3 blocker. Handoff includes changed paths, IDs, commands/results, fixture provenance, matrix gaps and next phase.
- Rechecked Java `NettyRemotingClient.invokeImpl` and `ChannelWrapper.reconnect`: the first GO_AWAY swaps the wrapper's current channel future but retains the old channel-to-wrapper association. Another pending request receiving GO_AWAY on that retired channel looks up the same wrapper and retries using the existing replacement; it does not reconnect/replace again. Rust previously failed this case. Changed `retire_for_go_away` to allow an already-retired generation to proceed through its invocation's single retry, which reacquires the address's currently cached connection. Reworked test into `p3_go_away_on_retired_connection_reuses_replacement_for_other_requests`: two old-connection requests both receive GO_AWAY and both complete successfully through a single replacement connection, without creating a second replacement. Targeted test passes. An invocation that itself receives GO_AWAY on its retry remains terminal due to `retried_go_away` guard.
- Updated compatibility inventory and test descriptions to reflect per-invocation retry on shared wrapper replacement rather than treating later GO_AWAY on an old generation as automatic failure. Other old-generation inbound response routing and pending-call tests remain.
- Full workspace test, Clippy, fmt, metadata, differential self-test, and diff-check gates passed before this GO_AWAY correction. They must all be rerun on final tree.
- User-accepted Netty/JVM framework API absences and specific Rust/Tokio differences are dispositioned; remaining PARTIAL rows and public/error/callback surfaces are still under source/race audit. No complete gate determination or commit yet; P3-V04 remains pending Phase 19. Historical untracked rlog `20260927T085045Z.rlog` left untouched.

